bucket type genral purpose and directory


1. General Purpose Bucket
Definition:
This is the standard way of creating an S3 bucket to store any kind of data (documents, images, backups, logs, etc.).

Key Points:

Flat storage — S3 doesn’t truly have “folders,” but uses a flat namespace.

Objects are stored with unique keys (like photos/2025/image1.jpg).

You can store millions of files without worrying about directory limits.

Best for most workloads — from static websites to application storage.

2. Directory-Style (Folder-Structured) Bucket
Definition:
This isn’t a different bucket type — rather, it’s an organizational method within the bucket.

Key Points:

You simulate folders using prefixes in object keys.

Example:

bash
Copy
Edit
invoices/2025/january.pdf
invoices/2025/february.pdf
Here, invoices/2025/ acts like a directory.

Useful for grouping related data for easier browsing in the AWS Console.

Prefixes also improve performance for some operations (like list filtering).
